**Read:** [Cleanroom programming and energy — Tschudi, Sartor and Xu](../sources/Cleanroom-Programming-and-Energy-LBNL.lingua#r_36R5RS6HEWBTYN4SHCVPQNP12X) and [Demand-controlled cleanroom filtration — Faulkner, Fisk and Walton](../sources/Demand-Controlled-Cleanroom-Filtration-Faulkner-Fisk-Walton.lingua#r_2W1ADE030W9RFA8ATDKSYVT6XP) in full; [Multizone airflow and contaminants — Dols and Polidoro, CONTAM 3.4](../sources/CONTAM-Multizone-Airflow-and-Contaminants-NIST.lingua#r_5HECDC480RSYAJ3SQ2QNFHCN46), control and transient theory.

Build a time-dependent account of process load, fans, thermal/moisture conditioning, utilities and operating states. Couple the physical state to the sensor's location, dynamics and recorded data. Evaluate required process conditions alongside energy and resource use.

**Work:** Reconstruct both reported energy baselines and independently check the hypothetical fan, heat and water balances. Compare two schedules with an explicit recovery condition. Investigate a delayed signal and a shared utility failure. Present a conclusion that survives changed occupancy, source strength and equipment loading, or state exactly where new evidence is needed.

